      "source": [
        "## 4. Results (vs baseline)\n",
        "*Model vs baseline on the same split. The honest table.*\n",
        "\n",
        "##**AUC** (test base rate: 0.418):\n",
        "\n",
        "| Method | AUC |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| Baseline (Week-4 rule) | 0.532 |\n",
        "| Logistic Regression | 0.521 |\n",
        "| Random Forest | 0.499 |\n",
        "\n",
        "All three sit essentially at chance (0.50). This is a major finding in itself: Week 5's earlier version of this comparison (before the clicks_feb >= 5 floor-effect fix) showed Random Forest reaching 0.842 AUC — that result was driven almost entirely by a mechanical artifact (pages with zero February clicks can never be labeled \"declined,\" which the model was exploiting rather than learning genuine content-decay signal). Once that artifact is removed, none of the three methods discriminate declining pages from stable ones meaningfully better than chance.\n",
        "\n",
        "##**Precision@K** (test base rate: 0.418):\n",
        "\n",
        "| K | Baseline | Logistic Regression | Random Forest |\n",
        "|---|---|---|---|\n",
        "| 20 | 0.15 | 0.80 | 0.10 |\n",
        "| 50 | 0.30 | 0.60 | 0.16 |\n",
        "| 100 | 0.40 | 0.50 | 0.38 |\n",
        "\n",
        "The k=20 numbers should not be trusted at face value: with only 12 clients in the test set, 14 of Logistic Regression's top 20 picks (70%) came from a single client. This is small-sample client concentration, not real ranking skill — consistent with the near-chance AUC for the same model. Precision@50 and @100 (larger, more diverse samples) sit close to the base rate for all three methods, which is the more trustworthy read and agrees with the AUC finding: no method meaningfully beats the base rate once the floor-effect fix is applied."
      ],
      "id": "Xk0I93e_giIl"
    },
